CELLS = {
    0: '# 07 · RAG — Contextual Chunking: Operating Protocol (Phase C-bis)\n\nThis notebook documents, with real code and real database execution outputs, the complete protocol followed to add **one context sentence per chunk** to the RAG corpus (Anthropic\'s *contextual retrieval* technique) using an external model (Gemini, via Antigravity) as a human-in-the-loop collaborator, without ever exposing it to the database or to the work already performed by Ollama.\n\n**Why this notebook exists**: it is not just a record of what was done, but a **reusable protocol** for future projects needing to delegate a massive generation task to an external LLM under a limited session budget, with automated verification and without requiring manual output inspection.\n\n**Sources:**\n- `methodology/ROADMAP_RAG_Y_GRAFO.md` (section 3, "Contextual chunking") and `methodology/ROADMAP_RAG_Y_GRAFO_v2.md` (component status).\n- [Contextual Retrieval in AI Systems — Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/contextual-retrieval)\n- `src/README.md` (entries `rag_export_context_batches.py`, `rag_import_context_batches.py`, `rag_export_context_repairs.py`, `rag_reembed_context.py`, `rag_query_compare.py`).\n- `data/rag_context_batches/README.md` (day-to-day operational cycle, gitignored, local-only).\n',
    2: '## 1. Why this component exists — what the roadmap states\n\nThe technique is not an original idea: it is *contextual retrieval*, published by Anthropic, and was cited in the project roadmap since version 1, marked as **pending implementation**. The literal quote (`ROADMAP_RAG_Y_GRAFO.md`):\n\n> *"Contextual chunking: before vectorizing, an inexpensive LLM appends 1-2 context sentences to each chunk [...]. It is Anthropic\'s technique — 35% fewer retrieval failures on its own."*\n\nAnd the result reported by Anthropic when combined with lexical search (BM25) and a reranker: **-67% retrieval failures** (from 5.7% to 1.9%). *Contextual embeddings* alone, without the other two components, already achieve **-35%**.\n\nThe critical detail for everything that follows: the technique specifies **"before vectorizing"**. Context is not stored alongside the vector as a decorative label — **it is merged with the chunk text, and THAT is what gets vectorized**. If not done this way, generating the sentences does not improve retrieval at all: the search engine only compares vectors and never reads auxiliary text stored alongside them.\n',
    3: '## 2. Architecture: isolating the external collaborator from validated work\n\nThree components, each with a very specific responsibility and write boundary:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    DOCS[("rag_documents<br/>1 row per PDF/paper, bibliographic catalog")]\n    CHUNKS["<b>rag_chunks</b> — 1 row per text fragment<br/>• text ← deterministic recursive splitter, NO LLM<br/>• embedding ← Ollama (bge-m3) vector of the chunk ALONE — Phase B, already closed<br/>• embedding_v2 ← Ollama vector of (context + chunk) — Phase D, new column, NEVER overwrites embedding"]\n    CTX["<b>rag_chunk_context</b> — sidecar table: ONLY the context sentence written by Gemini/Antigravity<br/>• context_sentence &nbsp; • source_model &nbsp; • batch_file<br/>No script in this phase ever writes to rag_chunks.text or rag_chunks.embedding"]\n\n    DOCS --> CHUNKS\n    CHUNKS -->|"chunk_id (FK)"| CTX\n\n    classDef prod fill:#E3F2FD,stroke:#1E88E5,color:#0D47A1\n    classDef side fill:#FFF3E0,stroke:#FB8C00,color:#E65100\n    class DOCS,CHUNKS prod\n    class CTX side\n```\n\n**Why a sidecar table and not a column directly in `rag_chunks`:** an external model, with an already known history of errors (see section 5), must never have a write path that touches a column on which production already depends (`embedding`). If the sidecar fails, it can be dropped and rebuilt without putting anything at risk. This same principle later justifies `embedding_v2` in section 7: not even the final step of vectorizing the context is allowed to overwrite the original.\n',
    5: '## 3. Step 1 — exporting batches by document, never by chunk\n\n`src/rag_export_context_batches.py` writes one JSON per document to `data/rag_context_batches/pending/`. The rationale for grouping by document (rather than by chunk) is the core premise of the technique itself: the model needs to see the **complete document** to situate each fragment within it — an isolated chunk has nothing against which to "situate itself". Grouping by document also reduces ~87,765 chunk-level requests down to ~956 document-level requests.\n\nThe privacy filter is a `WHERE` clause in the SQL query, not a prompt instruction that the model could potentially ignore:\n',
    7: '## 4. The prompt for Gemini / Antigravity\n\nThis is the exact prompt as pasted into the Antigravity chat at the start of each session, targeting the `pending/` folder. It already includes the reinforcement added after the first incident (section 5.1): the explicit instruction not to recycle sentences.\n',
    8: '> You will find JSON files in data/rag_context_batches/pending/ (or\n> data/rag_context_batches/repairs/pending/ for a repair batch). Each one\n> lists chunk_ids and the full text of the document they belong to. Follow\n> each file\'s own "instructions" field exactly — one specific,\n> non-generic sentence per chunk\\_id, grounded in what that exact chunk\n> says. Never a generic template, never a "(part N)" counter standing in\n> for a real difference, never a garbled or truncated word filling a gap,\n> and never the same sentence \\(or a near-identical one\\) reused across\n> multiple chunks. Process the files one at a time, in filename order,\n> moving each finished one to the matching done/ folder as you go. Stop\n> cleanly when pending/ is empty or you run out of session budget. Same\n> hard limits as always: never edit any .py file, never touch the\n> database, never open anything outside data/rag_context_batches/.\n\n**Autonomous** version (Antigravity with terminal access, running the full cycle autonomously in a loop until budget exhaustion or corpus completion — see section 6):\n\n> You also have permission to run terminal commands from the repository\n> root. Instead of stopping after one wave, run the full cycle in a loop:\n> 1. Run `python src/rag_export_context_batches.py --limit 30`. If it\n> exports 0 new files, the corpus is done — stop and report it. 2. Process\n> every file now in pending/, exactly as above, moving each to done/ as\n> you finish it. 3. Once pending/ is empty, run\n> `python src/rag_import_context_batches.py --apply`. 4. Run\n> `python src/rag_import_context_batches.py --status` and note the\n> numbers. 5. Repeat from step 1. Stop when: you run out of session\n> budget \\(finish the file you are on first\\), OR step 1 has nothing left\n> to export, OR a command errors. Hard limits: only ever run those two\n> exact commands, never raw SQL, never a database client, never edit any\n> .py file. If `--apply` reports rejected files, leave them in rejected/\n> untouched and just count them in your summary.\n',
    9: '## 5. Verify BEFORE writing — never trust that correct formatting implies correct content\n\n`src/rag_import_context_batches.py` never writes anything to `rag_chunk_context` without first passing through `verify()`. If a file fails, it is rejected **in its entirety** — never a partial document import — and moved to `rejected/` so the failure is loud rather than a silent gap discovered weeks later.\n',
    11: '### 5.1 Incident 1 — recycled sentences (the first real batch)\n\nThe first large batch processed by Gemini passed structural verification (same number of `chunk_id`, no missing gaps), but upon inspecting the content, it reused **3 to 10 generic sentences** for documents of 100-250 chunks — for example, 227 chunks with only 3 distinct sentences repeated in a loop. Nothing in rules 1-4 of `verify()` detected this because they were purely structural.\n\n**Fix**: rule 5 added to `verify()` — if a file has more than 5 results and fewer than 50% of the sentences are unique, it is rejected. Tested retroactively against the 30 files from the incident: **30/30 detected**, 0 false positives against valid files.\n\n### 5.2 Incident 2 — broken `"(part N)"` template\n\nA more subtle failure found *after* rule 5 was already in production: for a minority of chunks per document, the model filled a rigid template — `"This X chunk (part N) evaluates <broken fragment> regarding <title>"` — with a truncated or nonsensical word in the slot. Each sentence is **technically distinct** (the part number and fragment vary), so it passed the diversity rule without issue. It was only discovered by scanning already imported text for the pattern.\n',
    13: '### 5.3 Incident 3 — same issue as 5.1, but prior to the fix\n\nAuditing the entire corpus (not just recent batches) revealed **37 documents** with the original recycled sentences flaw — but their `created_at` was **prior** to the existence of rule 5, so they never underwent that validation. The filter did not fail; rather, an older slice of data fell outside its reach by design.\n\n**Lesson**: a check added to a pipeline only protects data processed *after* its deployment. Previously written data requires a separate retroactive audit — never assume "it is fixed now" covers the past.\n',
    15: '## 6. Surgical repair, not full corpus rebuilds\n\n`src/rag_export_context_repairs.py` provides two modes, one for each incident type, tailored to their scope within a document:\n\n- **`--mode template`** (Incident 5.2): within an affected document, only a minority of chunks were malformed (median <10%). Re-exporting the entire document would discard hundreds of valid sentences to fix a few. **Only flagged `chunk_id` values** are exported, providing the complete document text as context.\n- **`--mode recycled`** (Incident 5.3): in this case, most chunks within a document share a sentence, with no reliable way to identify which copy was "the authentic one" for a given chunk. The **entire document** is re-exported.\n\nBoth import using the standard `verify()`, targeting an isolated directory structure (`data/rag_context_batches/repairs/`) so repair tasks never mix with new document ingestion:\n',
    17: '## 7. Working in waves under a limited session budget\n\nAntigravity (the Google AI Pro subscription powering Gemini) has a session time budget. The protocol enabling safe interruption at any moment:\n\n1. `--status` before each wave: if `pending/` is not empty, uncompleted work from the previous session remains — resume processing that same folder; never export a new wave over it.\n2. `rag_export_context_batches.py --limit N` exports *only* documents lacking context sentences — repeating the command never duplicates work.\n3. If an Antigravity session terminates mid-run, completed work is safely stored in `done/`; remaining work stays in `pending/` awaiting the next session.\n4. `--apply` writes only entries that passed `verify()`; the rest route to `rejected/` without touching the database.\n\nFinal verified corpus status after resolving all three incidents:\n',
    19: '## 8. Phase D — from "stored sentence" to "retrieval-influencing sentence"\n\nCompleting the `rag_chunk_context` table **does not improve search on its own** — it is merely stored text. For Anthropic\'s technique to deliver its benefits, the step described by its name is required: vectorizing `context + chunk` together, not the chunk in isolation.\n\n**Explicit design decision**: this is written to a **new** column (`rag_chunks.embedding_v2`), never overwriting `embedding`. The rationale is straightforward: context sentences were generated by an external model with a documented history of errors (sections 5.1 to 5.3), and manually reviewing ~88,000 sentences is infeasible. With an isolated column, the worst-case rollback is `UPDATE rag_chunks SET embedding_v2 = NULL` — zero risk to existing production search.\n\n`src/rag_reembed_context.py` computes `embedding_v2` locally using the same Ollama model (`bge-m3`) employed for `embedding`, following the same batch and retry patterns as `rag_ingest_chunks.py`:\n',
    22: '### 8.1 A/B comparison — evaluating without sentence-by-sentence manual review\n\n`src/rag_query_compare.py` performs a pure cosine similarity search (without BM25 fusion) against both `embedding` and `embedding_v2` for the same query, printing results side by side. This allows validating whether context provides tangible improvements on real queries without inspecting all 88,000 generated sentences.\n\nReal output captured during phase development (pilot of 100 chunks from Salcedo-Abraira et al. 2023 on Cu MOFs for photocatalytic water splitting):\n\n```\nQuery: photocatalytic water splitting MOF\n==============================================================================\n\n--- WITHOUT context (embedding, currently used by rag_query.py) ---\n\n  1. similarity 0.7753   Wei Wang et al. (2017) DOI: 10.1002/advs.201600371\n     limited timescales, ranging from several hours to a few days. Robust\n     MOF-derived materials that can operate on a longer timescale are thus\n     needed to go a step further toward the real application of water\n     splitting. 3.2. Photocatalytic Water Splitting Some MOFs have shown\n     favorable photocatalytic activity for water split...\n\n--- WITH context (embedding_v2) ---\n\n  1. similarity 0.6321   Pablo Salcedo-Abraira et al. (2023) DOI: 10.1021/acs.chemmater.3c00054\n     Two Cu-Based Phosphonate Metal-Organic Frameworks as Efficient Water-\n     Splitting Photocatalysts...\n\nChunks appearing ONLY with context: 3\nChunks appearing ONLY without context: 3\nChunks in both                       : 0\n```\n\nWith only 100 vectorized chunks (from a single document), the comparison is not yet representative of the full corpus — this represents pilot validation confirming end-to-end mechanism integrity, not a comprehensive quality benchmark. True evaluation occurs once `embedding_v2` is complete (section 8, progress table) by querying domain-specific benchmarks and comparing retrieved passages.\n\n**Important**: cosine similarity scores for `embedding` and `embedding_v2` cannot be directly compared in absolute value (prepending context shifts vector coordinates in bge-m3 space) — evaluation must focus on whether retrieved **passages** are more relevant to the query, not whether numerical similarity scores are higher or lower.\n',
    23: "## 9. Generalizable protocol — checklist for future projects\n\nKey takeaways from this phase, applicable beyond ALCheMy's RAG as a reusable pattern for large-scale external LLM delegation:\n\n1. **Privacy filters belong in SQL `WHERE` clauses, never in prompt instructions.** A model can miss an instruction; it cannot access a row the query never returned.\n2. **Use a sidecar table, not a new column in production tables**, while the external collaborator generates data. This isolates worst-case failures.\n3. **Verify BEFORE writing, and reject the entire batch unit** (never partial writes) on failure — loud errors are cheap to fix; silent gaps are discovered weeks later.\n4. **Structural checks (valid format) do not guarantee content validity.** Incident 5.1 demonstrated that well-formed files can contain low-quality output. Both layers are mandatory.\n5. **Pipeline checks only protect future processing.** Retroactively auditing pre-existing pipeline data is an explicit, necessary step (Incident 5.3).\n6. **Apply surgical repairs when defects are sparse; reprocess the entire unit when defects dominate.** The approach depends on whether defective segments can be reliably isolated.\n7. **Batch by the unit requiring full context** (here, document), not the smallest granular unit (chunk) — even if it increases prompt size.\n8. **File-based handoff + status commands** when external tools have session limits. Never rely on long sessions completing without interruption.\n9. **When final output populates production embeddings, write to a new column and run A/B query benchmarks** before replacing existing versions. Manually auditing thousands of AI-generated entries is unfeasible — comparative retrieval evaluation is practical and reliable.\n",
}
